1
00:00:04,250 --> 00:00:07,245
Synthetic data, from a spec or from a profile.

2
00:00:08,045 --> 00:00:14,474
Pulse can write a cohort as well as read one. Pulse synth writes a dot pulse file, and it has two modes.

3
00:00:15,274 --> 00:00:18,034
From schema takes a spec you write by hand.

4
00:00:18,834 --> 00:00:23,151
From profile tops up a real cohort with new rows, each one tagged.

5
00:00:23,951 --> 00:00:29,250
Both run from the command line or the library. There is no M C P tool for synth.

6
00:00:30,050 --> 00:00:35,818
Here is a spec: two thousand rows and seven fields, each with a type and a distribution.

7
00:00:36,618 --> 00:00:40,701
Seed twenty-two: two thousand rows generated, none rejected.

8
00:00:41,501 --> 00:00:46,544
The ids come from a counter that ignores the random stream: clean primary keys.

9
00:00:47,344 --> 00:00:52,280
Read the file back with ordinary requests. The regions land near their weights.

10
00:00:53,080 --> 00:01:01,451
Age stays inside its clamp. Visits average two point nine three against a lambda of three, and thirty-five percent of rows are members.

11
00:01:02,251 --> 00:01:05,800
Synth draws, so a figure lands near its target, not on it.

12
00:01:06,600 --> 00:01:17,552
Today's distributions include normal, lognormal, Poisson, Bernoulli, categories, dates, regex and mixtures. More are added over time, and the manifest lists them.

13
00:01:18,352 --> 00:01:27,960
Some carry rules you cannot guess: discrete values ascend, a mixture needs two or more components, and set Bernoulli registers its options up front.

14
00:01:28,760 --> 00:01:34,059
The spec is strict: rate on a Poisson field is refused, and lambda is suggested.

15
00:01:34,859 --> 00:01:40,307
Fields are drawn independently by default: age and spend correlate at about zero.

16
00:01:41,107 --> 00:01:53,275
A correlations entry names a pair and a rho. Pulse draws correlated normals, turns them into probabilities, and reads each through its field's own quantile function: a Gaussian copula.

17
00:01:54,075 --> 00:02:06,008
Each field keeps its own distribution. The rank correlation lands near zero point five nine; Pearson's r is lower, near zero point five five, because spend is lognormal.

18
00:02:06,808 --> 00:02:12,491
A Poisson field cannot join. And a pair you leave out is drawn independent, with a warning.

19
00:02:13,291 --> 00:02:21,576
A constraint rejects a row, and the generator redraws it. Requiring spend of at least thirty rejects two hundred and forty draws.

20
00:02:22,376 --> 00:02:28,933
The file still holds two thousand rows, and the ids have no gaps: the counter counts accepted rows.

21
00:02:29,733 --> 00:02:35,821
Cap the rejection rate at ten percent, and the run fails as infeasible. No file is written.

22
00:02:36,621 --> 00:02:44,416
Rules act on a drawn row. When picks the rows. Set writes a literal, set expr an expression, and set null masks a field.

23
00:02:45,216 --> 00:02:52,627
Rules run last, in order, and the last write wins. Here every non-member loses their tier, and every member keeps one.

24
00:02:53,427 --> 00:03:00,048
V I P is derived from spend. A rule that never fires, here ages over ninety, gets a warning.

25
00:03:00,848 --> 00:03:06,808
Rules are checked before any row is drawn: nulling a field that is not nullable is refused.

26
00:03:07,608 --> 00:03:15,787
When relationships name the same field, Pulse settles them once, in a fixed order: rules, models, shapes, pairs, then correlations.

27
00:03:16,587 --> 00:03:21,779
This rule sets spend on every row, so it claims spend ahead of the copula, with a warning.

28
00:03:22,579 --> 00:03:29,670
Age and spend now correlate at zero point zero two. The first claim wins, and every loser is reported.

29
00:03:30,470 --> 00:03:35,491
Rows are generated in order and never shuffled, so a rule can read earlier rows.

30
00:03:36,291 --> 00:03:44,747
Row gives the row's number, and lag reads a field n rows back. Here stock carries sixty percent of last week's stock: an adstock.

31
00:03:45,547 --> 00:03:50,632
Week one: thirty-two point two two, plus point six times eighty-one point five.

32
00:03:51,432 --> 00:03:55,216
Neither draws randomness, and a profile never captures them.

33
00:03:56,016 --> 00:04:05,475
Profile create reads a real cohort: shares for categories, histograms for small integers, moments for the rest, and the strongest correlations.

34
00:04:06,275 --> 00:04:13,984
From profile adds five hundred rows. The source is copied through, tagged synthetic false, and the new rows are tagged true.

35
00:04:14,784 --> 00:04:20,019
The source is never opened for write. Point the output at it, and the run is refused.

36
00:04:20,819 --> 00:04:27,931
A fidelity report scores the new rows against the source: a K S test for numbers, chi-square for categories.

37
00:04:28,731 --> 00:04:39,918
Region, age and plan pass. Spend fails: it was rebuilt as a clamped normal, and the source is skewed. The correlation comes back close, at zero point four four nine.

38
00:04:40,718 --> 00:04:49,537
With fit shape, spend becomes a mixture and passes. But the shape claims spend first, so its correlation is dropped, and reported, not scored.

39
00:04:50,337 --> 00:04:57,065
Synth is deterministic. Same spec, same seed: the file is byte-identical, and the hashes match.

40
00:04:57,865 --> 00:05:03,356
Seed twenty-three gives a different file. Seed zero is a fixed seed, not a random one.

41
00:05:04,156 --> 00:05:07,663
Profile capture keeps the same promise, byte for byte.

42
00:05:08,463 --> 00:05:15,810
The cohorts in this series are not made with synth. They come from seeded generators, or small hand-written C S Vs.

43
00:05:16,610 --> 00:05:22,143
The generators use a port of Python's random module, so each one rebuilds byte for byte.

44
00:05:22,943 --> 00:05:33,212
Synth can make the same kind of cohort: this film topped up the generator's members with five hundred rows from Pulse. The brand tracker case study is built with synth.

45
00:05:34,012 --> 00:05:43,364
That is synth: specs and profiles, distributions and the copula, constraints and rules, earlier rows, fidelity, and the same bytes for the same seed.

46
00:05:44,164 --> 00:05:51,980
Synth is not private: a profile reveals the source's distributions. Add calibrated noise when the source is sensitive.

47
00:05:52,780 --> 00:06:00,148
More distributions are added over time, and the manifest lists what your instance offers. Next up, embedding Pulse.

48
00:06:00,948 --> 00:06:02,791
Pulse. Synthetic data.
